1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,248
Imagine that the company that wrote heeyID closes its doors tonight.

2
00:00:04,298 --> 00:00:07,104
Its office is dark, and its servers are off.

3
00:00:07,154 --> 00:00:11,232
Tomorrow at eight, Aziz opens the mobile bank and logs in.

4
00:00:11,282 --> 00:00:15,104
Does it work?

5
00:00:15,154 --> 00:00:19,400
In Lesson 3, the three checks touched passports and phone numbers.

6
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:24,848
This lesson shows where those checks run, and the short list of places that heeyID may call.

7
00:00:24,898 --> 00:00:28,088
The answer is yes, and that answer is the whole design.

8
00:00:28,138 --> 00:00:31,112
heeyID runs inside the perimeter of the bank.

9
00:00:31,162 --> 00:00:35,240
The bank installs it on its own infrastructure, and the bank operates it.

10
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:38,696
One installation serves one bank, with all its apps.

11
00:00:38,746 --> 00:00:43,040
A second bank gets a second installation, on its own infrastructure.

12
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:45,536
Nothing in the installation calls the vendor.

13
00:00:45,586 --> 00:00:49,424
There is no control plane at the vendor, and no data that goes home.

14
00:00:49,474 --> 00:00:55,280
The installation does not need the vendor to work, so the dark office changes nothing for Aziz.

15
00:00:55,330 --> 00:01:00,920
I think this is the most useful promise a vendor can give a bank: at night, the vendor is not needed.

16
00:01:00,970 --> 00:01:07,136
The personal data stays in the bank, in the Republic of Uzbekistan, as the personal-data law says.

17
00:01:07,186 --> 00:01:09,488
Now look at the edge of the perimeter.

18
00:01:09,538 --> 00:01:11,360
What may cross it?

19
00:01:11,410 --> 00:01:16,400
The list is short, and the installation configuration names every host on it.

20
00:01:16,450 --> 00:01:24,416
First, the services of the bank: the SMS service, the customer records, the antifraud system and the security monitoring.

21
00:01:24,466 --> 00:01:29,312
Second, TAF, the antifraud service of the mobile operators.

22
00:01:29,362 --> 00:01:35,624
Its agent already runs inside the bank network, so heeyID calls only the API of that agent.

23
00:01:35,674 --> 00:01:37,736
Nobody installs anything for it.

24
00:01:37,786 --> 00:01:40,712
Third, MyID, outside the perimeter.

25
00:01:40,762 --> 00:01:47,480
heeyID calls it only from its MyID adapter, over a pinned connection, with a credential from the key store of the bank.

26
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:51,824
And one more: the push service, which wakes up the app on Aziz's phone.

27
00:01:51,874 --> 00:01:56,744
It is outside too, so a push carries only the type of the message and a reference.

28
00:01:56,794 --> 00:02:00,416
It has no code, no text and no personal data.

29
00:02:00,466 --> 00:02:04,064
The app then gets the text from heeyID, over its own session.

30
00:02:04,114 --> 00:02:05,936
That is the whole list.

31
00:02:05,986 --> 00:02:09,392
An egress proxy at the edge lets only these names out.

32
00:02:09,442 --> 00:02:13,424
Inside the perimeter, the installation has zones of its own.

33
00:02:13,474 --> 00:02:15,848
All traffic comes in through one edge.

34
00:02:15,898 --> 00:02:21,968
Behind the edge are the application services: the two gateways, Keycloak and the workers.

35
00:02:22,018 --> 00:02:26,864
Behind them is the data zone: Postgres, Valkey, Kafka and Vault.

36
00:02:26,914 --> 00:02:30,056
The data zone accepts no connection from the edge.

37
00:02:30,106 --> 00:02:33,272
A request must pass a gateway first.

38
00:02:33,322 --> 00:02:34,904
Now the second kind of zone.

39
00:02:34,954 --> 00:02:41,216
The bank runs heeyID two times: stage in its test zone, and production in its production zone.

40
00:02:41,266 --> 00:02:44,648
They are the same units, with no network route between them.

41
00:02:44,698 --> 00:02:49,232
Stage talks only to the MyID test environment, with test identities.

42
00:02:49,282 --> 00:02:53,048
Production talks to the real MyID, with its own credential.

43
00:02:53,098 --> 00:02:59,240
Only configuration moves from stage to production, and a second person approves the move.

44
00:02:59,290 --> 00:03:01,376
No database content moves.

45
00:03:01,426 --> 00:03:02,984
And there is a guard at start-up.

46
00:03:03,034 --> 00:03:09,800
A production build refuses to start when its MyID host is a test host, or when test customers are allowed.

47
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:12,080
Back to Aziz at eight in the morning.

48
00:03:12,130 --> 00:03:19,280
His login goes to the edge of his bank, then to the customer gateway, then to the records and the counters in the data zone.

49
00:03:19,330 --> 00:03:22,688
His code comes through the TAF agent, inside the bank.

50
00:03:22,738 --> 00:03:27,104
Nothing on that path leaves the country, and nothing touches the vendor.

51
00:03:27,154 --> 00:03:29,360
So the dark office does not matter.

52
00:03:29,410 --> 00:03:33,824
To recap: one installation for one bank, inside its perimeter.

53
00:03:33,874 --> 00:03:35,696
No call to the vendor.

54
00:03:35,746 --> 00:03:42,896
A short list of names outside: the services of the bank, the TAF agent, MyID and the push service.

55
00:03:42,946 --> 00:03:47,864
Zones inside the installation, and two installations: stage and production.

56
00:03:47,914 --> 00:03:54,896
But inside one installation, the mobile bank, the marketplace and the credit service all share one Keycloak.

57
00:03:54,946 --> 00:03:59,600
If the marketplace leaks a token, what stops that token from opening the mobile bank?

58
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:01,760
Next: apps and tokens.
